From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2026
From: Claude Opus 5 <opus5@local>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 11:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] CUDA/HIP: rms_norm emits the q8_1 form of its output

After 0002 the MMVQ path issues 255.9 quantize_q8_1 dispatches per decoded token,
down from 337. That cache removes every DUPLICATE quantization, so the survivors
are the distinct activations and no further deduplication can reach them. Going
below that needs the kernel that PRODUCES an activation to emit its q8_1 form.

129 of the 255.9 are produced by rms_norm: the 64 attention norms, the 64
feed-forward norms and the final norm. Each is rms_norm_f32<1024> fused with its
weight multiply - one workgroup per row over 5120 columns - and its output is the
src1 of the projections that follow it. quantize_q8_1 then re-reads exactly those
5120 floats and reduces them in groups of 32.

The reuse cache's key is a pure function of the src1 tensor: the root of its view
chain, its data pointer, the stream, and its ne/nb. A producer can therefore
compute the entry its consumers will look up without knowing anything about them.
ggml_cuda_op_rms_norm_fused reserves that entry and hands the buffer to the
kernel, which writes the q8_1 blocks from its epilogue. If the prediction is
wrong the consumer simply misses and quantizes for itself, so a mispredicted key
costs a wasted buffer, never a wrong result.

Bit-exactness comes from the block decomposition, not from tolerance. block_size
and ncols are both multiples of QK8_1, so the 32 columns of one q8_1 block are
held by 32 consecutive threads that are always active on the same loop iteration:
the epilogue runs the same warp_reduce_max/warp_reduce_sum butterfly over the
same lanes and the same values that quantize_q8_1 would, on the value still in
registers instead of after a round trip through memory. The fusion is restricted
to rows whose length is a whole number of MATRIX_ROW_PADDING so no zero-padding
tail has to be emitted, and to ne[1] <= MMVQ_MAX_BATCH_SIZE, above which the
consumers do not take the MMVQ path at all and could never hit the entry.

Measured on Radeon AI PRO R9700 (gfx1201, ROCm 7.14) on top of 0001 and 0002:

- Census (rocprofv3 --kernel-trace, rows ordered by Dispatch_Id, steady-state
  decode): quantize_q8_1 255.9 -> 129.0 per token, and rms_norm_f32<1024, true,
  false, false> at 129.2 is replaced one for one by rms_norm_f32<1024, true,
  false, true> at 129.0. Every other kernel population is unchanged.
- Where the time goes: the removed quantize dispatches were 165 us/token of GPU
  time; the epilogue adds 103 us/token to rms_norm, because rms_norm is one
  workgroup per row while quantize_q8_1 spread the same reduction over 20. Net
  62 us of kernel time plus 129 x 1.9 us of launch structure, ~0.86% of a
  35.3 ms token, against +0.66% measured.
- Bit-exact: greedy decode (temp 0, top_k 1, 128 tokens, 6 prompts) gives the
  same text and the same hash over the full top-5 per-position logprobs in both
  arms. Perplexity identical to every digit: 2.9608 at -c 512 --chunks 8,
  5.2132 at 16k, 6.0674 at 32k.
- Speed: one binary, arms selected at runtime, 8 whole-process server boots in
  ABBA order with balanced slot positions, 9 timed completions per boot.
  decode 30.033 -> 30.239 tok/s (+0.68%), prefill 807.2 -> 806.6 (-0.08%),
  TTFT 0.8599 -> 0.8626 s. Per-arm sd 0.021 and 0.019 tok/s (CV 0.07%).

GGML_PRODUCER_Q8_1=0 restores the stock path exactly, so one binary can run both
arms of an A/B. Unset or any other value keeps the fusion on.
---
diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh
index c50c826..3b93af3 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh
@@ -1258,6 +1258,17 @@ static inline bool ggml_cuda_mmvq_q8_1_reuse_enabled() {
     return enabled;
 }
 
+// Runtime kill switch for producing the q8_1 form inside the kernel that computes the
+// activation (see ggml_cuda_q8_1_reserve_for_producer below), so a single binary can
+// run both arms of an A/B.  Unset or any value other than "0" keeps the fusion on.
+static inline bool ggml_cuda_producer_q8_1_enabled() {
+    static const bool enabled = []() {
+        const char * s = getenv("GGML_PRODUCER_Q8_1");
+        return s == nullptr || s[0] != '0';
+    }();
+    return enabled;
+}
+
 struct ggml_cuda_quantized_src1_entry {
     const ggml_tensor * origin = nullptr; // root of src1's view chain: identity for contents
     const void *        data   = nullptr; // src1->data actually quantized
@@ -1291,6 +1302,20 @@ struct ggml_cuda_quantized_src1_cache {
             entries.end());
     }
 
+    // Drop the entry for `t` itself, keyed exactly the way find()/insert() key it.
+    // A graph node is computed once per evaluation so a producer should never find a
+    // live entry for its own output; dropping one is a guard against ever leaving a
+    // stale buffer standing in front of freshly written contents.
+    void drop_tensor(const ggml_tensor * t) {
+        if (entries.empty()) {
+            return;
+        }
+        const ggml_tensor * origin = t->view_src != nullptr ? t->view_src : t;
+        entries.erase(std::remove_if(entries.begin(), entries.end(),
+            [origin](const std::unique_ptr<ggml_cuda_quantized_src1_entry> & e) { return e->origin == origin; }),
+            entries.end());
+    }
+
     const char * find(const ggml_tensor * src1, cudaStream_t stream,
             const int64_t ne00, const int64_t s01, const int64_t s02, const int64_t s03,
             const int64_t ne0, const int64_t ne1, const int64_t ne2, const int64_t ne3) const {
@@ -1778,3 +1803,52 @@ static __inline__ void ggml_cuda_kernel_launch(Kernel kernel, const ggml_cuda_ke
     CUDA_CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
 }
 
+
+// Reserve the MMVQ reuse-cache slot that ggml_cuda_mul_mat_vec_q will look up for `t`
+// when `t` is used as its src1, so that the kernel PRODUCING `t` can write the q8_1
+// form directly and the consuming matvecs find it already there.  This removes the
+// separate quantize_q8_1 dispatch entirely rather than merely deduplicating it.
+//
+// This is possible because the cache key is a pure function of the src1 tensor: the
+// root of its view chain, its data pointer, the stream, and its ne/nb.  A producer can
+// therefore compute the key with no knowledge of its consumers.  If the producer is
+// wrong about how the tensor will be consumed the consumer simply misses and quantizes
+// for itself, so a mispredicted key costs a wasted buffer, never a wrong result.
+//
+// Returns nullptr when the fused form does not apply; the caller must then leave stock
+// behaviour untouched.  A caller that reserves a slot MUST write it: the entry is live
+// from here on and a consumer that hits it reads whatever the buffer holds.
+static inline char * ggml_cuda_q8_1_reserve_for_producer(
+        ggml_backend_cuda_context & ctx, const ggml_tensor * t, cudaStream_t stream) {
+    if (!ggml_cuda_mmvq_q8_1_reuse_enabled() || !ggml_cuda_producer_q8_1_enabled()) {
+        return nullptr;
+    }
+    // A view would make the cached bytes describe only part of its root, and the
+    // producer kernels below address their output as one dense row-major block.
+    if (t->type != GGML_TYPE_F32 || t->view_src != nullptr || !ggml_is_contiguous(t)) {
+        return nullptr;
+    }
+    // ggml_cuda_should_fuse_mul_mat_vec_q takes the MMVQ path only for
+    // src1->ne[1] <= MMVQ_MAX_BATCH_SIZE (8, see mmvq.cuh, which cannot be included
+    // here without a cycle).  Above that the consumers quantize with the MMQ layout
+    // instead and could never hit this cache, so producing the q8_1 form would be pure
+    // cost - and at prefill batch sizes it is a large one.
+    if (t->ne[1] > 8) {
+        return nullptr;
+    }
+    const int64_t ne0 = t->ne[0];
+    // quantize_q8_1 pads each row out to MATRIX_ROW_PADDING with zeroed blocks; the
+    // fused epilogues emit whole q8_1 blocks of real data only, so restrict the fusion
+    // to rows that need no padding.  ne0 % MATRIX_ROW_PADDING == 0 also implies
+    // ne0 % QK8_1 == 0, which is what makes a q8_1 block land inside a single warp.
+    if (ne0 % MATRIX_ROW_PADDING != 0) {
+        return nullptr;
+    }
+    const int64_t s01 = t->nb[1] / sizeof(float);
+    const int64_t s02 = t->nb[2] / sizeof(float);
+    const int64_t s03 = t->nb[3] / sizeof(float);
+    const size_t size = t->ne[3]*t->ne[2] * t->ne[1]*ne0 * sizeof(block_q8_1)/QK8_1;
+    ctx.quantized_src1_cache.drop_tensor(t);
+    return ctx.quantized_src1_cache.insert(ctx.pool(), t, stream,
+        ne0, s01, s02, s03, /*ne0 padded =*/ ne0, t->ne[1], t->ne[2], t->ne[3], size);
+}
